  "en": {
    "title": "Your first 7 steps",
    "description": "Five sentences at a time: read the pattern, say your answer, then check the text and audio. This is a suggested order; you can start anywhere.",
    "intro": [
      {
        "title": "Start with sound and reading",
        "body": "Use the romanization alongside the recording. म विद्यार्थी हुँ (ma vidyārthī hũ) means “I am a student.” Reveal the answer in practice, listen and say it aloud."
      },
      {
        "title": "Notice a and ā",
        "body": "a and ā represent different vowel qualities; ā is the more open vowel. The bars in ī and ū also preserve spelling distinctions. Do not assume every bar means exactly twice the length: follow the recording."
      },
      {
        "title": "Listen for the extra breath",
        "body": "Pairs such as k/kh and t/th differ in aspiration. With kh, release an extra puff of air after k. th is not English “th.” A dot in ṭ or ḍ marks a tongue position farther back than t or d."
      },
      {
        "title": "Some vowels have a nasal quality",
        "body": "The tilde in यहाँ (yahā̃, “here”) marks a nasal vowel. Let the vowel resonate through the nose; do not simply add a separate n. Listen for this in हुँ (hũ, “I am”) too."
      },
      {
        "title": "Build from the ending",
        "body": "म विद्यार्थी हुँ follows “I + student + am.” Verbs usually come at the end, but their forms change with person and respect. Begin with म (I), हामी (we), तपाईं (you) and उहाँ (he/she, respectful), then learn the roles of हो, छ and हुन्छ."
      }
    ],
